# Probe: the gemvs' `_prefetch` entries (each warp asks L2 for its whole row's lines before its walk,
# cores::l2_prefetch) on the discrete RTX 5090, where row_prefetch picks them only on an integrated device. The
# research round's rank 3 (llama.cpp mmvq.cu ggml_cuda_prefetch_l2, enabled there on DGX Spark only). A prefetch
# costs issue slots (one per 128-byte line a lane: a 2880-byte Q4_K row is 23 lines, under one pass of a warp) and
# no registers it keeps; if the walk's loads then hit L2, the open requests drain faster. The 3090-era comment says
# it costs more than it hides on a discrete card; the 5090's GDDR7 latency is the unknown [derived]; a loss reads as decide-below.
kind: ab
question: do the row-prefetch gemv entries speed a full-card decode step on the RTX 5090?
term: decode tok/s @ depth 512, Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M whole on the RTX 5090, generate_qwen3moe of the q4v4 tree with
  row_prefetch forced on against the q4v4 binary, (probe - q4v4) / q4v4
unit: %
predict: +0.3..+2.0
rounds: 6
sd: 0.05 % docs/cards/nqfold-split.card and shortk-ba-fold.card outcomes, paired round-ratio SD 0.014 % and 0.017 % over 6 rounds each, rounded up threefold
condition: just depth-gpu-qwen35moe with both arms at 512@model=/models/Qwen3.8-27B/Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf in one
  lease, the base as a bin: arm; the 5090 alone at 600 W
decide-in: above +0.3: make the pick a card fact (a lever row and the 5090's default), gates on both entries
decide-below: under +0.3: the prefetch does not pay on GDDR7; keep the integrated-only pick
decide-above: above +2.0: read nsys before crediting
# outcome 2026-11-21, pf-bin (q4v4 tree, row_prefetch forced on; a37119e3429a) against v4-bin (4edeb091e27d), RTX 5090
#  600 W, one lease, 6 rounds, depth 512: decode 83.92 tok/s [83.90..83.94] against 86.70 [86.68..86.71], round
#  ratios 0.96808 0.96781 0.96802 0.96794 0.96773 0.96792 = -3.21 %. decide-below: the row prefetch costs on GDDR7 as
#  on the 3090; the integrated-only pick stays.
